column14:
@kseniaistratova
The best aspect was working with amazing people who were always kind, nice, and supportive. It was a fantastic opportunity to learn from others and share knowledge.

@valpolarbear
We worked great as a team. Everybody helped each other.

@nishavijai
Best aspect - Meeting a wonderful team and working together, learning new techniques from the code, our team have worked on to build this project. One of the best team, I have worked so far among all the other Chingu Voyages. No negative vibes, no pressure/stress, mutual respect and understanding of everyone's coding skill. Our team agreed to submit the project with what we have worked so far. Moreover, our team would like to continue working on the same web app on next our voyage to make a better web app. Extremely grateful to work with our team. 

@seiya2323
For me, the best part of this team experience was working collaboratively and getting a taste of how real-world companies develop an app, from design and stories to tickets, development, and production. Learning how to use Git effectively and staying updated with everyone’s work was super nice.

@kyndrill
The best part was the team--everyone was so helpful, kind, and fun to work with.  I think we all brought different experiences and skillsets, which meant we could learn from each other and create something amazing!

@brockdev
I really liked the ability to choose any project that we wanted to pursue.  It was helpful to start communicating before sprint 1 so we has a good idea of what we wanted to accomplish and defined our roles. Everyone brought unique strengths and I am grateful that I learned something from everyone.  Personally, I felt more confident in working with our git flow, creating better PRs, approving and understanding the importance of making "micro" pulls and pushes to better avoid merge conflicts.  Understanding the big picture of how backend meets front end was very helpful

@sharafcs50
The design, implemented Auto Layout on Figma which was a new experience for me, got to learn a lot

@deepali7555
The most rewarding part was collaborating with an incredible team. I thoroughly enjoyed our team meetings and gained valuable insights while working on various tasks. Everyone was so supportive and understanding, making it one of the best teams I've ever had the pleasure of working with.

column15:
@kseniaistratova
The hardest part was working with React JS and sometimes struggling to stay focused on the overall picture of the project while trying to prioritize the most valuable functionalities.

@valpolarbear
It was the best team that I worked with in Chingu so far.

@nishavijai
Hardest aspect - I could not communicate with the team, due to time zone challenge. It would be even more best experience to talk to entire team, while working on a project. Apart from this, this is my first full stack (tier-3) voyage. I could not read more about AuthO and Redux toolkit integration with AuthO was tough to work on in the past one week. I am glad to come across what I can and can not do in one week. Found a way to make a temporary solution to submit the project.

@seiya2323
The most challenging aspect of this project was not being able to attend as many meetings due to my schedule. However, I was persistent this time and feel like I participated more. For the next Voyage, I hope to attend more meetings if they align with my schedule. I’m usually free at night when I’m back on my PC, but my daily runs after work often clashed with the meeting times this Voyage. 

@kyndrill
Personally, this time my biggest problem was finding enough time to participate.  If I had had enough time, I think the hardest part would have been continuing to learn technologies I am not very familiar with, but even though that is hard it's super rewarding.

@brockdev
Personally it was ironic that as a school teacher with summers off, I felt most distracted during this voyage.  In hindsight, when I have the structure and routine of working and going to my workplace everyday, I schedule work times throughout my day and actually am more productive.  A wide open schedule creates a greater demand for domestic responsibilities.  Also, I thought I would have been more available during my travels to contribute, so thanks for everyone's flexibility and understanding during this last sprint.
Thanks to everyone!

@sharafcs50
Planning for the dashboard after login screen which we sorted out with the team effort.

@deepali7555
The most challenging aspect was working on the front-end in React, as I didn't have prior experience with it. However, thanks to the team's support and guidance, I was able to complete my tasks on time.

column16:
@kseniaistratova
A big thanks to the Chingu Admin team for providing the opportunity to create and work in this great team. Without Chingu, we wouldn’t have met each other.

@valpolarbear
Thank you everybody!  

@nishavijai
Additional comments - I would like to use the entire sentence from Ksusha & additionally, I would like to thank Ksusha, who helped me to work on this team & Val to agree to have me on the team & the entire team who have been supportive & positive comments on Git and here on channels. Overall pleased to meet you all and looking forward to work with you all in the upcoming voyages.

@seiya2323
I’d also like to thank Chingu and Val for inviting me to be a part of this experience, because of that, I met all you wonderful people. It’s been incredibly worthwhile, and I now feel much more confident with the Git workflow, as well as collaborating and working on tasks.

@kyndrill
This has been a wonderful experience and I hope I can work with this team again someday when my life has settled down a bit!  I am amazed by what we created in such a short time.

@brockdev
Thanks to everyone!

@sharafcs50
It was a good team effort and all the developers worked hard in coordination with the design team.

@deepali7555
I would like to thank the entire team and Chingu for giving me the opportunity to work on this project.

